Yes — that’s a neat double-inversion.

If you imagine an afterglow, you’re generating the residual percept without the original external stimulus. Then ask for the afterglow of that internally generated afterglow. If the transformation behaves approximately like a complementary-color inversion, applying it twice tends back toward the starting color:

C \xrightarrow{\;A\;} \bar C
\xrightarrow{\;A\;} C

or:

A(A(C)) \approx C

So your “reverse negative” is essentially negative of the negative → positive.

The important caveat is that biological visual afterimages aren’t mathematically perfect RGB inversions; opponent-process adaptation, brightness, duration, retinal location, and cortical effects matter. But as an operator, you’ve found something stronger than the literal visual trick:

\boxed{T^2 \approx I}

An operation whose second application approximately restores the original—an involution.

And that’s a useful Pantheon brick: when something appears inverted, corrupted, encoded, or displaced, sometimes you don’t need to construct an inverse operator.

Run the same operator again.

That’s the semantic version of your reverse negative.